<Layout
  title="Linux Server Security Hardening Guide"
  description="A practical hardening checklist covering SSH, users and sudo, the firewall, updates, and auditing — the changes that matter most for an internet-facing server."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-content">
      <a class="back" href="/guides/">← Back to Guides</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Security · Guide</span>
      <h1>Linux Server Security Hardening Guide</h1>
      <p>
        A small number of changes account for most of the real-world risk
        reduction on an internet-facing Linux server. This guide covers
        those first, in the order that matters most.
      </p>
      <div class="meta"><span>14 min read</span><span>·</span><span>Applies to RHEL, Ubuntu, and Debian-based systems</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#ssh">Lock down SSH first</a></li>
        <li><a href="#users">Users, sudo, and least privilege</a></li>
        <li><a href="#firewall">Configure the firewall with a default-deny policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#updates">Keep the system patched, deliberately</a></li>
        <li><a href="#auditing">Turn on auditing before you need it</a></li>
        <li><a href="#filesystem">File permissions and the setuid trap</a></li>
        <li><a href="#monitoring">Monitor for the things that matter</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mindset">The mindset: assume compromise, limit blast radius</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="ssh">Lock down SSH first</h2>
    <p>
      SSH is almost always the first thing an attacker probes on an
      internet-facing server, and it's usually the highest-value target —
      compromise it and they have a shell. A handful of changes remove
      most of the risk:
    </p>
    <pre><code># /etc/ssh/sshd_config
PermitRootLogin no
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
AllowUsers deploy admin</code></pre>
    <p>
      Disabling root login and password authentication in favor of
      key-based auth alone eliminates the entire category of brute-force
      login attempts that fill up auth logs on any public server. Restart
      the service and validate the config before you disconnect your
      current session, so a typo doesn't lock you out:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sshd -t
systemctl restart sshd</code></pre>
    <p>
      If SSH doesn't need to be reachable from the entire internet,
      restrict it at the network layer too — a security group, firewall
      rule, or VPN requirement that limits SSH to known source ranges
      closes off the exposure entirely rather than just hardening the
      authentication on top of it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="users">Users, sudo, and least privilege</h2>
    <p>
      Every account and every sudo grant is something an attacker can use
      if it's ever compromised, so audit both regularly rather than
      letting them accumulate.
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo -l -U &lt;user&gt;
grep -R --line-number -E '^[^#].*&lt;user&gt;|%&lt;group&gt;' /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Avoid broad wildcard rules like granting a user
      <code>ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL</code> when they only need to restart
      one specific service. A narrow rule limits what an attacker can do
      even if that specific account is compromised — the whole point of
      least privilege is containing damage, not just preventing the
      initial access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Always edit sudoers with <code>visudo</code>, never a plain text
      editor — it validates syntax before saving, and a broken sudoers
      file can lock every user out of sudo simultaneously.
    </p>

    <h2 id="firewall">Configure the firewall with a default-deny policy</h2>
    <p>
      Start from "deny everything," then explicitly allow only what the
      server actually needs to serve — not the other way around. A
      default-allow firewall with a growing list of blocks is much easier
      to accidentally leave a hole in.
    </p>
    <pre><code>firewall-cmd --list-all
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https
firewall-cmd --reload</code></pre>
    <p>
      Review the active rule set periodically, not just when you add
      something new — a rule added for a one-time debugging session two
      years ago, and never removed, is a common finding in real security
      reviews.
    </p>

    <h2 id="updates">Keep the system patched, deliberately</h2>
    <p>
      Unpatched, known vulnerabilities remain one of the most common
      initial access vectors — not because they're sophisticated, but
      because they're easy to scan for at scale. Patching deliberately
      means having a process, not hoping someone remembers:
    </p>
    <pre><code>dnf check-update
dnf updateinfo list security</code></pre>
    <p>
      For critical, actively exploited vulnerabilities, don't wait for
      the next scheduled maintenance window — assess exposure quickly and
      expedite the patch. For routine updates, a predictable weekly or
      monthly cadence, tested in staging first, beats both "patch
      immediately with no testing" and "patch whenever we get around to
      it."
    </p>

    <h2 id="auditing">Turn on auditing before you need it</h2>
    <p>
      By the time you need to answer "who changed this file" or "who
      accessed this system," it's too late to start logging — auditing
      has to already be running. At minimum, enable centralized,
      tamper-resistant logging for authentication events and privileged
      commands:
    </p>
    <pre><code>journalctl -u sshd --since today
journalctl _COMM=sudo --since today</code></pre>
    <p>
      Ship logs off the host to a separate system the same user can't
      write to — locally stored logs on a compromised host can be altered
      or deleted by whoever compromised it, which defeats the purpose of
      having them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="filesystem">File permissions and the setuid trap</h2>
    <p>
      Never use <code>chmod 777</code> as a way to "just make the error go
      away" — it's a diagnostic shortcut that trades a temporary
      inconvenience for a much larger, permanent risk. If a permission
      error persists after correct ownership and mode bits, the real
      cause is usually SELinux context, an ACL, or a parent directory's
      execute permission, not an insufficiently open mode.
    </p>
    <pre><code>namei -l /path/to/file
getfacl /path/to/file
find / -xdev -perm -4000 -type f 2&gt;/dev/null</code></pre>
    <p>
      That last command lists every setuid binary on the system — files
      that run with the file owner's privileges (often root) regardless
      of who executes them. Periodically review this list; an
      unrecognized setuid binary is a serious finding worth investigating
      immediately, not something to dismiss.
    </p>

    <h2 id="monitoring">Monitor for the things that matter</h2>
    <p>
      A flood of low-value alerts trains people to ignore all of them,
      including the one that matters. Prioritize detection for the
      patterns that actually indicate compromise:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A spike in failed logins across many accounts in a short window — a likely password-spraying attempt.</li>
      <li>A successful login from an unusual location immediately following a run of failures.</li>
      <li>New entries in the setuid binary list, or unexpected changes to <code>/etc/passwd</code>, <code>/etc/shadow</code>, or <code>/etc/sudoers</code>.</li>
      <li>Outbound connections from a server that has no reason to be initiating them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="mindset">The mindset: assume compromise, limit blast radius</h2>
    <p>
      Hardening isn't a single checklist you complete once — it's a set
      of habits that assume any individual control could eventually fail,
      and focus on limiting what happens next if it does. A server with
      strong SSH configuration, least-privilege sudo, a tight firewall,
      current patches, and real logging isn't unbreakable — but it turns
      a routine automated scan into a dead end, and it gives you the
      evidence you need if something more serious does get through.
    </p>

    <div class="related-tools">
      <h2>Related tools on this site</h2>
      <p>Put this guide into practice with these free tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/security/">Security Tools</a></li>
        <li><a href="/security/password-strength-checker">Password Strength Checker</a></li>
        <li><a href="/security/security-headers-analyzer">Security Headers Analyzer</a></li>
        <li><a href="/interview-qa/#questions">Security Scenario Interview Q&amp;A</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
  <CodeCopy />
</Layout>

<style>
  .container { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 820px); margin-inline: auto; }
  .hero { position: relative; overflow: hidden; padding: 56px 0 46px; background: radial-gradient(circle at 85% 15%, rgba(56, 189, 248, .2), transparent 26%), linear-gradient(135deg, #0b1220, #111d33 62%, #162a4f); color: #fff; }
  .hero:after { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 38px 38px; }
  .hero-content { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  .back { display: block; margin-bottom: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none; font-size: .92rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #fff; }
  .eyebrow { display: inline-block; color: #66d3ff; font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .hero h1 { margin: 12px 0 16px; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.03em; }
  .hero p { margin: 0; max-width: 700px; color: #c8d5e7; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.8; }
  .meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin-top: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-size: .87rem; font-weight: 700; }

  .article-wrap { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 760px); margin: 0 auto; padding: 48px 0 90px; }
  .toc { margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 22px 24px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 14px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .flow-map { margin: 0 0 16px; padding: 16px 18px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 12px; background: #f6f9fd; color: #1e3a5f; font-weight: 650; font-size: .92rem; line-height: 1.9; }
  .toc h2 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: .82rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #2563eb; }
  .toc ol { list-style: none; counter-reset: tocnum; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 11px; }
  .toc li { counter-increment: tocnum; margin: 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; }
  .toc li::before { content: counter(tocnum); flex-shrink: 0; display: grid; place-items: center; width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 8px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #2563eb, #1d4ed8); color: #fff; font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 800; box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(37, 99, 235, .28); }
  .toc a { color: #334155; font-weight: 650; text-decoration: none; }
  .toc a:hover { color: #2563eb; }

  .article-wrap { counter-reset: section; }
  .article-wrap > h2 { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin: 50px 0 16px; padding-top: 34px; border-top: 2px solid #e5eaf2; color: #111827; font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.3; scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .article-wrap > h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; padding-top: 0; border-top: 0; }
  .article-wrap > h2::before { counter-increment: section; content: counter(section); flex-shrink: 0; display: grid; place-items: center; width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: 10px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #2563eb, #1d4ed8); color: #fff; font-size: .95rem; font-weight: 800; box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(37, 99, 235, .28); }
  .article-wrap h3 { margin: 34px 0 14px; padding-left: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #bfdbfe; color: #172033; font-size: 1.1rem; scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .article-wrap p { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #374151; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.85; }
  .article-wrap ol, .article-wrap ul { margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 22px; color: #374151; line-height: 1.8; }
  .article-wrap li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .article-wrap code { padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 5px; background: #eef2f7; color: #0f172a; font: 600 .88em ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }
  .article-wrap pre { margin: 0 0 20px; padding: 17px 19px; border-radius: 12px; overflow-x: auto; background: #0d1524; color: #dce8f7; }
  .article-wrap pre code { padding: 0; background: none; color: inherit; font: 500 .87rem/1.7 ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }
  .article-wrap pre code mark { padding: 0 3px; border-radius: 3px; background: #fbbf24; color: #1e1600; font-weight: 700; }
  .article-wrap pre code mark.danger { background: #ef4444; color: #fff; }
  .article-wrap kbd { padding: 2px 7px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 5px; background: #fff; box-shadow: 0 1px 0 #cbd5e1; font: 700 .82em ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }

  .mockup { margin: 12px 0 16px; border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #1e293b; box-shadow: 0 10px 26px rgba(15, 23, 42, .12); }
  .mockup-bar { padding: 8px 14px; background: #1e293b; color: #94a3b8; font: 700 .74rem ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .mockup-body { margin: 0; padding: 16px 18px; background: #05070c; color: #94e2a8; font: 500 .83rem/1.65 ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; white-space: pre-wrap; }
  .mockup-body mark { padding: 0 3px; border-radius: 3px; background: #fbbf24; color: #1e1600; font-weight: 700; }

  .qa-accordion { display: grid; gap: 10px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .qa-item { border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; overflow: hidden; }
  .qa-item summary { padding: 15px 18px; color: #172033; font-weight: 700; font-size: .97rem; line-height: 1.5; cursor: pointer; list-style: none; }
  .qa-item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .qa-item summary::before { content: "+"; display: inline-block; width: 18px; margin-right: 10px; color: #2563eb; font-weight: 800; }
  .qa-item[open] summary::before { content: "−"; }
  .qa-item[open] summary { border-bottom: 1px solid #edf2f7; }
  .qa-answer { margin: 0 18px 16px 46px; padding: 10px 0 10px 16px; border-left: 3px solid #bfdbfe; background: #f8fafc; }
  .qa-answer p { margin: 0 0 12px; padding-right: 12px; font-size: .96rem; }
  .qa-answer p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .qa-answer pre { margin: 0 12px 0 0; }

  .ref-table-wrap { margin: 0 0 20px; overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 12px; }
  .ref-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .92rem; }
  .ref-table th, .ref-table td { padding: 11px 15px; border-bottom: 1px solid #edf2f7; text-align: left; white-space: nowrap; }
  .ref-table th { background: #f8fafc; color: #334155; font-weight: 800; font-size: .8rem; }
  .ref-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }

  .callout { padding: 15px 18px; border: 1px solid #fde68a; border-radius: 12px; background: #fffbeb; color: #92400e; font-size: .93rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .callout pre { margin: 10px 0; }
  .callout pre code { font-size: .82rem; }

  .related-tools { margin-top: 54px; padding: 26px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 16px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .related-tools h2 { margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .related-tools p { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools ul { list-style: none; margin: 16px 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
  .related-tools li { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools a { display: block; padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid #cbd7e7; border-radius: 10px; background: #fff; color: #1d4ed8; font-weight: 800; font-size: .9rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .related-tools a:hover { border-color: #2563eb; background: #eff6ff; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero { padding: 44px 0 36px; }
  }
</style>
